ct**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The problem requires a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n2. **Necessary condition.**  \n   The three “corner’’ points  \n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,1),\\;P_{2}=(n,1),\\;P_{3}=(1,n)\n   \\]\n   cannot be collinear with a sunny line, because a sunny line is never horizontal, vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of \\(\\{P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\}\\).  \n   To cover the three points we need at least three lines, and at most one of them can be sunny.  \n   Consequently **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.  \n   \\[\n   N:=n-k\\ge 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Two fixed non‑sunny lines*  \n   \\[\n   L_{v}:x=1\\qquad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad   \n   L_{h}:y=1\\qquad(\\text{horizontal}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines covering the remaining points.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the lattice point  \n   \\[\n   Q_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\in T_{n}.\n   \\]\n\n   - If \\(n\\) is **even**, for every \\(t\\) the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\(Q_{t}\\) has slope\n     \\[\n     m_{t}= \\frac{n-t}{t-1},\n     \\]\n     which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Thus each such line is sunny, and the \\(n-2\\) lines\n     \\[\n     L_{t}:=\\text{line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }Q_{t}\\qquad(2\\le t\\le n)\n     \