he following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have a complete solution.  \nThe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary condition.**  \n   The three “corner’’ points \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) cannot all lie on a sunny line, because a sunny line is never horizontal, vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(n-k\\ge 2\\) and therefore \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n2. **Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Two fixed non‑sunny lines*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:x=1\\qquad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad   \n   L_{h}:y=1\\qquad(\\text{horizontal}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   Q_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\in T_{n},\n   \\qquad\n   L_{t}:=\\text{the line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }Q_{t}.\n   \\]\n\n   - If \\(n\\) is **even**, the slope of \\(L_{t}\\) is  \n\n     \\[\n     m_{t}= \\frac{n-t}{t-1},\n     \\]\n\n     which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n     Hence each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny, and the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\) are distinct sunny lines.\n\n   - If \\(n\\) is **odd**, let  \n\n     \\[\n     t_{0}= \\frac{n+1}{2}.\n     \\]\n\n     For \\(t\\neq t_{0}\\) the same formula gives a sunny line \\(L_{t}\\).  \n     For \\(t=t_{0}\\) the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\(Q_{t_{0}}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore not sunny.  \n     Replace it by the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{t_{0}}':\\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }Q_{t_{0}},\n     \\]\n\n     whose slope is \\(-1+\\dfrac{2}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n     Thus we again obtain \\(n-2\\)