n at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not horizontal, not vertical, and not of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (necessary condition)\n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(1,n)\n\\]\n\ncannot all belong to a sunny line, because a sunny line is never horizontal, never vertical, and never of slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently at least two of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non‑sunny; hence  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nDefine two fixed non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}:x=1,\\qquad L_{h}:y=1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nQ_{t}=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\in T_{n}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Even \\(n\\).**  \nFor every \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:=\\text{line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }Q_{t}\n\\]\n\nhas slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{t}= \\frac{n-t}{t-1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\), we have \\(0<m_{t}<\\infty\\) and \\(m_{t}\\neq -1\\); thus each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\{L_{t}\\mid 2\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\n**Odd \\(n\\).**  \nLet \\(t_{0}= (n+1)/2\\). For \\(t\\neq t_{0}\\) the same definition of \\(L_{t}\\) yields a sunny line.  \nFor \\(t=t_{0}\\) the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\(Q_{t_{0}}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is not sunny.  \nReplace it by  \n\n\\[\nL_{t_{0}}':=\\text{line through }(1,2)\\text{ and }Q_{t_{0}} .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-t_{0}-2}{t_{0}-1}= -1+\\frac{2}{n-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_{t_{0}}'\\) is sunny and distinct from all other \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nThus in both parity cases we have exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_{v},L_{h}\\), giving a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n---\n\n**Covering verification.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_{v}\\);  \n* if \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_{h}\\);  \n* if \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\((a,b)=Q_{a}\\).  \n  For even \\(n\\) it lies on \\(L_{a}\\); for odd \\(n\\) it lies on \\(L_{a}\\) unless \\(a=t_{0}\\), in which case it lies on \\(L_{t_{0}}'\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n---\n\n**Obtaining fewer sunny lines.**  \nStarting from the above family (which has \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines), choose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=(n-2)-k\\).  \nFor each \\(t\\in S\\) replace the sunny line \\(L_{t}\\) by the vertical line \\(x=t\\) (which is non‑sunny).  \nThe point \\(Q_{t}\\) remains covered (now by the new vertical line), and all lines stay distinct.  \nAfter all replacements we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\ge2\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nSince \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), every such \\(k\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nBy Lemma 1 we must have at least two non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nTherefore the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) together give the complete description of admissible \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case v