) points with pairwise distinct abscissae (or distinct sums \\(a+b\\)), allowing us to perform the replacement up to \\(n\\) times.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines in (2) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A useful auxiliary fact  \n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(P\\in T_n\\). There exists a line \\(\\ell\\) passing through \\(P\\) whose slope is irrational. Consequently \\(\\ell\\) contains **no other point** of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Choose any irrational number \\(\\alpha\\).  The line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y-b=\\alpha (x-a)\n\\]\n\npasses through \\(P=(a,b)\\).  If \\((x',y')\\in T_n\\) also lay on \\(\\ell\\) then  \n\n\\[\ny'-b=\\alpha (x'-a).\n\\]\n\nSince the left–hand side is an integer and \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, the only possibility is \\(x'=a\\) and \\(y'=b\\).  Hence \\(\\ell\\cap T_n=\\{P\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one  \n\nFix a point \\(P\\in T_n\\).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a non‑sunny line that contains \\(P\\) (for instance a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\)).  \n\n*Construction.*  \n1. Draw, by Lemma 1, a sunny line \\(\\ell_P\\) through \\(P\\) that mee