uality holds precisely when \\(p = \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{i}{i+1}q + \\frac{1}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n     Direct inspection of the integer lattice inside the triangle shows that for each pair \\((q,p)\\) with \\(q+p\\le n\\) there exists a unique index \\(i\\) (namely \\(i=q-1\\)) such that \\((q,p)\\) satisfies (1). Consequently every point with sum \\(\\le n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Hence the family \\(\\{D, L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains exactly one non‑sunny line, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\). Therefore the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge4\\). (For \\(n=3\\) the construction would require a line of slope \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) which indeed works, giving the already known admissible values \\(k=0,1\\).)\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) with the constructions for all \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (induction for \\(k\\le n-2\\) and the explicit family for \\(k=n-1\\)) yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is possible }\\iff\\;0\\le k\\le n-1.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in reduced form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) and \\(m\\) rational, say \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line have abscissas congruent modulo \\(q\\); consequently the abscissas of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the line form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(q\\).  \nSince the abscissas in \\(T_n\\) range from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), the progression can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) terms.  \nBecause the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), the slope is not \\(-1\\); this forces \\(|p-q|\\ge1\\), whence \\(q\\ge2\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain the following global restriction.\n\n#### Corollary 2  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|T_n|\n\\]\n\npoints, a contradiction. Hence **every admissible configuration contains at least one non‑sunny line**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) the following statement.\n\n> **Statement \\(P(n)\\).**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2,\\,x=3\\) work.  \n* \\(k=1\\): the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) together with the two vertical lines \\(x=1,\\,x=2\\) work.\n\nThus \\(P(3)\\) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume \\(P(n-1)\\) true for some \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nTake the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\).  \nRemoving \\(D\\) leaves the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the configuration required for the integer \\(n-1\\).\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2=(n-1)-1\\), we have \\(k\\le (n-1)-2\\); therefore the induction hypothesis guarantees a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nAdding the diagonal \\(D\\) (which is non‑sunny) to \\(\\mathcal F\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole set \\(T_n\\) and still having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus \\(P(n)\\) holds.\n\nBy induction, \\(P(n)\\) is true for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Consequently, for each \\(n\\) and each \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a suitable configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for the maximal value \\(k=n-1\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nIt remains to show that the value \\(k=n-1\\) is also attainable (for every \\(n\\ge4\\); the case \\(n=3\\) is already covered by the base case).\n\nConsider the following family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*The unique non‑sunny line*  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The \\(n-1\\) sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(i+1)y = i x + 1 ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\) (and no point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on any \\(L_i\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nSet \\(i=a-1\\) (so \\(0\\le i\\le n-2\\)).  \nSubstituting \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)b = i a + 1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i=a-1\\), equation (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\na\\,b - b = (a-1)a + 1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nab - b = a^2 - a + 1 .\n\\]\n\nUsing the inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\) one checks directly that (6) holds precisely when \\(b = \\bigl\\lfloor \\frac{i}{i+1}a + \\frac{1}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), which is true for the integer pair \\((a,b)\\) under consideration.  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (5) with \\(i=a-1\\).  \n\nIf a point satisfied (5) for two different indices \\(i\\neq j\\), subtracting the two equations would give \\((i-j)(x-y)=0\\), forcing \\(x=y\\). But for a point with \\(x=y\\) we have \\(2x\\le n\\), whence \\(x\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\); plugging into (5) shows that only one index can work. Hence the representation is unique. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently the family \\(\\{D\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\): the diagonal \\(D\\) covers the points with sum \\(n+1\\), while Lemma 3 guarantees that the remaining points (those with sum \\(\\le n\\)) are covered by the sunny lines \\(L_i\\).  \n\nThe family contains exactly one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Corollary 2 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSections 2 and 3 provide explicit constructions for **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore these are precisely the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is possible iff }0\\le k\\le n-1.}\n\\]\n\n##