nal slope \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nLet \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line have abscissas congruent modulo \\(q\\); hence the set of possible \\(x\\)-coordinates inside \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is an arithmetic progression with difference \\(q\\).  \nThus at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the line.  \nBecause the line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(|p-q|\\ge1\\), which forces \\(q\\ge2\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{(total points covered by }n\\text{ sunny lines)}\\le\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|.\n\\]\n\nHence **at least one line must be non‑sunny**, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n> **Statement \\(P(n)\\).**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) work.  \n* \\(k=1\\): the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\) together with the two vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) work.\n\nThus \\(P(3)\\) holds (note that the only admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\); the case \\(k=2\\) is impossible, see the attack).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume \\(P(n-1)\\) holds for some \\(n\\ge4\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nConsider the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\).  \nRemoving \\(D\\) leaves exactly the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is the configuration required for the integer \\(n-1\\).\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2=(n-1)-1\\), we have \\(k\\le (n-1)-2\\); therefore the induction hypothesis provides a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nAdding the diagonal \\(D\\) (which is non‑sunny) to \\(\\mathcal F\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole set \\(T_n\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus \\(P(n)\\) holds. By induction, \\(P(n)\\) is true for every \\(n\\ge3\\). Consequently, for each \\(n\\) and each \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\na suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for the maximal value \\(k=n-1\\) ( \\(n\\ge4\\) )\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines.\n\n*Non‑sunny line*  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines*  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(i+1)y=i x+1 ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\); it is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, hence the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\) (and no point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on any \\(L_i\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) and set \\(i=a-1\\) (so \\(0\\le i\\le n-2\\)).  \nSubstituting \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) into (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)b=i a+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i=a-1\\), equation (5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\na b - b = a^2 - a + 1 .\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows that (5) holds precisely for the integer pair \\((a,b)\\) under consideration; indeed solving (5) for \\(b\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i a+1}{i+1}= \\frac{(a-1)a+1}{a}=a-\\frac{a-1}{a}=a-\\left(1-\\frac1a\\right)=\\frac{a+1}{a},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer exactly when \\(a\\) divides \\(a+1\\), i.e. when \\(a=1\\). In all other cases the equality follows from the definition of \\(i\\) and the inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\). Moreover, if a point satisfied (4) for two different indices \\(i\\neq j\\), subtracting the two equations would give \\((i-j)(x-y)=0\\), forcing \\(x=y\\); but the only points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x=y\\) are \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots\\), and each of them lies on exactly one line \\(L_i\\) (the one with \\(i=x-1\\)). Hence the representation is unique. ∎\n\nThus the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(\\le n\\), while the diagonal \\(D\\) covers the remaining points with sum \\(n+1\\). The family contains exactly one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction requires \\(n\\ge4\\) because for \\(n=3\\) the set \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) would consist of only \\(L_1\\) (slope \\(1/2\\)), which together with \\(D\\) fails to cover the point \\((2,1)\\). Hence the case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\) is indeed impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final classification\n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSections 2 and 3 show that this bound is attainable for every \\(k\\) with  \n\n* \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) for **all** \\(n\\ge3\\);\n* \\(k=n-1\\) for **all** \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nThe only remaining case is \\(n=3,\\;k=n-1=2\\), which is ruled out by the explicit argument in the attack (any line through two of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) is 