}\\) splits as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{P}_{\\,n}= \\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-1}\\;\\cup\\;\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The added line \\(\\ell_{n}\\) contains precisely the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n}^{(k)}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n}\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell_{n}\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it is a legitimate non‑sunny line, and it is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) (all those lines intersect the triangle only in points with \\(a+b\\le n\\)).  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n}^{(k)}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which exactly the same \\(k\\) lines are sunny as in \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\).\n\n   By induction, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required family of lines exists.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) and the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) together with the constructive proof above show that the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that we must find \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\mathcal{L}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal{L}} L\\supseteq \\mathcal{P}_{n},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*, i.e. their slopes are different\nfrom \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Trivial bounds\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny\nlines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{3}\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nA short inspection yields the following families:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=3\\}\\) (all three lines are non‑sunny);\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=2x-1\\}\\) (the third line has slope \\(2\\));\n* \\(k=2\\): \\(\\{x=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=3x-2\\}\\);\n* \\(k=3\\): three arbitrary lines with slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) is realizable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\) the following statement holds:\n\n> (**Induction hypothesis**)  \n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family\n> \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) for\n> \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{n}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is **not**\nsunny.  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_{n}^{(k)}:=\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\cup\\{\\ell_{n}\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n}^{(k)}\\) meets all requirements.\n\n* **Cardinality.**  \n  \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) contains \\(n-1\\) lines; adding \\(\\ell_{n}\\) yields\n  exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Distinctness holds because every line of\n  \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) meets the triangle only in points with\n  \\(a+b\\le n\\), whereas \\(\\ell_{n}\\) consists solely of points with\n  \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n* **Coverage of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).**  \n  The set \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) splits as  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal{P}_{n}= \\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-1}\\;\\cup\\;\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n  By the induction hypothesis, \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\) covers\n  \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-1}\\).  The added line \\(\\ell_{n}\\) contains precisely the\n  points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Consequently the union of all lines in\n  \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n}^{(k)}\\) covers the whole \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n* **Number of sunny lines.**  \n  The only new line is \\(\\ell_{n}\\), which is non‑sunny.  Hence the number of\n  sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n}^{(k)}\\) equals the number of sunny lines in\n  \\(\\mathcal{L}_{\\,n-1}^{(k)}\\), namely \\(k\\).\n\nThus for the same integer \\(k\\) we have constructed a suitable family of\n\\(n\\) lines for \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  The induction step is complete.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies the required families for all\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  By the inductive argument, for every\n\\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nCombining the trivial bound (2) with the constructive existence proved\nabove, we obtain that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\nprecisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThus for each non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the two conditions of the problem, and no\nother values of \\(k\\) are possible. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
